  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\n\nOur approach combines multi-resolution optimization with coordinate-wise updates to minimize the maximum correlation. We use adaptive projective feature mapping to escape local optima and maintain spacing between peaks. The core rationale is to:\n\n- Utilize hierarchical optimization: start with a coarse grid (n=64) and upsample to higher resolution (n=500)\n- Employ projective grid transformation that preserves key moments between resolutions\n- Implement adaptive windowing for correlation calculation to focus optimization on significant patterns\n- Combine coordinate-wise and gradient-domain updates\n- Use multi-scale exploration with early convergence detection\n\nThe key limitations addressed are:\n\n- The fixed grid size in the original algorithm might restrict exploration of alternative patterns\n- Over-reliance on p-norm optimization in coordinate directions\n- Limited handling of multi-resolution structure\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n\nThe objective is to minimize:\n    max_k |\u222c h(x)[1-h(x+k)] dx|   (continuous domain)\nDiscretized as:\n    dx_max = 1/(max_grid_size)   (we optimize grid sizes)\n    C = max_k (sum_{i,j} h[i] * (1-h[j]) * dx^2) * dx_max    (approximates the integral)\n\nConstraints:\n    0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1\n    sum_i h[i] * dx = 1\n\nThe optimization involves:\n\n- Coarse solution generation with projective feature mapping\n- Multi-scale grid transformation: Px(t) = t * f(x) + (1-t)*g(x)\n- Coordinate-wise updates with penalty constraints\n- Gradient projection:\n     grad C = max_{k} { \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx } * sign(correlation)\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n\nPhase 1: Initialization\n- If initial_h_values available: use it as coarse parent\n- Project initial sample to [0,1] and [0,1]^2 space manually\n\nPhase 2: Multi-resolution cascade\nfor resolution in [64, 320, 500]:\n    a. Grid transformation:\n        current_grid = coarse_grid if first pass else interpolate\n        X = resample(current_grid, resolution)\n        Y = project_features(X)   # Custom feature projection (adaptive)\n\n    b. Optimization loop with adaptive c1, c2:\n        penalized_C() = max_k( correlate(X) ) + c1*(1-sum(X)^2) + c2*sum(|X|)^p\n        update_parameters() via exact_cd with:\n            n_iterations = min(200, (deadline - time())/10)\n            grid_points = int(15 + 30 * (resolution/500))\n\n        project_features(x):\n            return tanh(x) + exp(-x^2) + (resolution)/6\n\n    c. Internal projectors:\n        clip_bounds(X)\n        normalize_sum(X)\n\nPhase 3: Hierarchical continuation\n    Cascade from low to high resolution with:\n        - Coarse-to-fine interpolation\n        - Feature transfer with scaling factor t=0.75\n        - Add new features at higher resolution:\n            X_high = X_low * (xi coefficient) + (1-X_low)*gaussian_knots\n\nPhase 4: Random restarts:\n    Within 40% of budget, perform random jitter with:\n        X_jitter = X + (rng.random(10)-0.5)*0.05 * (penalty factor)\n\n4. Search choices and variations:\n\nGrid progression: [64, 320, 500]\n\nKey hyperparameters:\n    c1_c2 = [0.01, 0.1, 1.0]   # Progressive feature projection scaling\n    p_exponents = [1.2, 1.8, 2.5]  # At each resolution\n    grid_multiplier = [2, 2, 1]   # Coarse to fine steps\n\nMonitoring:\n- Early termination: if improvement stalls for 3 consecutive iterations\n- Grid change: trigger when current score > resolution^2/1000\n- Validation: recompute objective on original grid if NaN detected\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness:\n\nTime allocation:\n- Coarse (64): 40s\n- Medium (320): 500s\n- Fine (500): 360s\n- Validation: 50s during fine phase\n\nResource handling:\n- dx scaling automatically based on resolution\n- Validate at each grid change and every 30 seconds\n- Fallback: if invalid solution detected, use project_feasible()\n\nError recovery:\n- Use tanh projection if bounds violations\n- Apply gradient clipping if gradient grows beyond 100\n- Maintain 5 best candidates throughout optimization\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n\nFinal checks:\n- compute_c5(h, n)\n- clip_bounds with lists\n- validate_integral(h): abs(sum(h)*(2/n)-1 < 1e-4\n- sanitize_output(h): remove NaNs, project\n\nReturns:\n- Tuple (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)\n\nError handling:\n- If invalid score at any grid level, default to interpolation of parents\n- Catch and ignore correlation overflow (value > 15)\n- Use median of last 10 evaluations to prevent runaway values\n\nThe algorithm leverages multi-resolution optimization while maintaining the core constraints. The adaptive projective transformation allows exploration of feature spaces not accessible by standard optimization.",
